    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "2. Adjectives that change meaning"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "With ser the adjective describes a <b>permanent quality</b>; with estar it describes a <b>state, result or impression</b>."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        3.0,
        6.4,
        7.6
      ],
      "head": [
        "Adjective",
        "SER",
        "ESTAR"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "listo",
          "ser listo = to be clever",
          "estar listo = to be ready"
        ],
        [
          "aburrido",
          "ser aburrido = to be boring",
          "estar aburrido = to be bored"
        ],
        [
          "malo",
          "ser malo = to be bad (character)",
          "estar malo = to be ill / to taste bad"
        ],
        [
          "bueno",
          "ser bueno = to be good (kind)",
          "estar bueno = to taste good / be attractive"
        ],
        [
          "rico",
          "ser rico = to be rich",
          "estar rico = to taste delicious"
        ],
        [
          "vivo",
          "ser vivo = to be sharp, quick",
          "estar vivo = to be alive"
        ],
        [
          "seguro",
          "ser seguro = to be safe",
          "estar seguro = to be sure"
        ],
        [
          "cansado",
          "ser cansado = to be tiring",
          "estar cansado = to be tired"
        ],
        [
          "verde",
          "ser verde = to be green (colour)",
          "estar verde = to be unripe"
        ],
        [
          "abierto",
          "ser abierto = to be open-minded",
          "estar abierto = to be open (not closed)"
        ],
        [
          "orgulloso",
          "ser orgulloso = to be proud (arrogant)",
          "estar orgulloso = to be proud (of something)"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "<b>Examples:</b> <i>Mi primo es muy listo. / ¿Estás listo? Nos vamos.</i> · <i>El profesor es aburrido. / Estoy aburrido.</i> · <i>Ese chico es malo. / Estoy malo: tengo fiebre.</i>"
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "spain",
      "title": "Estar bueno / estar rico",
      "text": "In Spain <b>está buena</b> / <b>está bueno</b> about a person means \"attractive\" (informal). About food use <b>está rico</b> or <b>está bueno</b>: <i>Este jamón está buenísimo.</i>"
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "3. Expressing change"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Dutch uses <i>worden</i> for all changes. Spanish chooses a verb depending on the kind of change."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        3.2,
        5.6,
        5.0,
        3.2
      ],
      "head": [
        "Verb",
        "Use",
        "Example",
        "Followed by"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "ponerse",
          "temporary change: mood, colour, health",
          "Me puse nervioso. Se puso rojo.",
          "adjective"
        ],
        [
          "volverse",
          "sudden or deep change of character",
          "Se volvió loco. Se ha vuelto muy callado.",
          "adjective"
        ],
        [
          "hacerse",
          "change through effort or choice (job, religion, rich)",
          "Se hizo médico. Me hice vegetariano.",
          "noun / adjective"
        ],
        [
          "convertirse en",
          "complete transformation",
          "El pueblo se convirtió en ciudad.",
          "noun"
        ],
        [
          "quedarse",
          "result of an event (lasting state)",
          "Se quedó calvo. Me quedé sin dinero.",
          "adjective / sin"
        ],
        [
          "llegar a ser",
          "gradual achievement",
          "Llegó a ser director.",
          "noun"
        ],
        [
          "acabar + gerund",
          "to end up",
          "Acabé comprando el rojo.",
          "gerund"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>Emotions</b> with reflexive verbs: <i>enfadarse, alegrarse, preocuparse, aburrirse, enamorarse, sorprenderse</i>.",
        "<b>Ponerse + adjective</b> is the most common: <i>Cuando oyó la noticia, se puso muy triste.</i>",
        "<b>Hacerse</b> also means to become older: <i>Me estoy haciendo mayor.</i>",
        "Time-related: <i>hacerse de noche</i> (to get dark), <i>hacerse tarde</i> (to get late)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "worden",
      "text": "Dutch <i>hij werd boos</i> = <b>se puso furioso</b> (temporary mood). <i>Hij werd arts</i> = <b>se hizo médico</b>. <i>Hij werd gek</i> = <b>se volvió loco</b>. Always ask: temporary, effort or sudden?"
    },
